ave the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Yakutat has set <strong class=\"sig\">32 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 94 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">141 record highs</strong>, far more than the 94 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (73). Yakutat's all-time high is 88\u00b0F, on August 15, 2004.","t_chg":"Yakutat has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 93 complete years: Yakutat's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.00 to +0.31), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1918. Yakutat's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.27\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.39), about +2.9\u00b0F since 1918.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 4 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Yakutat's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.64 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 139.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 1987 (250.24 in); the driest, 1950 (85.99 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 3 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}